# Findings

Significant findings from the April 2026 assessment, grouped by host. Severity uses the Nessus output (CVSS v3 where reported). EPSS and VPR values are quoted where Nessus surfaced them and were notable.

---

## Metasploitable 2 — `172.16.20.100`

This host is intentionally vulnerable and serves as the deliberate target of the assessment. Findings below illustrate categories of risk; the host itself is not intended to be hardened.

### F-MS2-01 — UnrealIRCd Backdoor (Critical, CVSS 10.0)

| Field | Value |
| ----- | ----- |
| CVSS  | 10.0 (Critical) |
| VPR   | 7.4 |
| EPSS  | 0.856 |
| Port  | TCP 6667 |

**What it is.** UnrealIRCd 3.2.8.1 was distributed with a malicious backdoor that grants arbitrary command execution to an unauthenticated remote attacker.

**Why it matters.** Bypasses authentication entirely. A reachable, vulnerable instance is functionally equivalent to handing the attacker a shell.

**Impact.** Full host compromise. The backdoored service runs as a privileged process, so post-exploitation is unconstrained.

**Mitigation.** Remove the backdoored binary, upgrade to a clean UnrealIRCd release from a trusted source, and rebuild the host if compromise is suspected. For the lab, the existence of the vulnerability is the point — no mitigation applied.

---

### F-MS2-02 — Remote Shell via Default / Weak Credentials (Critical, CVSS 10.0)

| Field | Value |
| ----- | ----- |
| CVSS  | 10.0 (Critical) |
| Affected services | Apache Tomcat manager, VNC, Telnet, FTP, others |

**What it is.** Multiple services accept default or trivial credentials (e.g., `tomcat:tomcat`, VNC `password`, etc.). Several services also run in cleartext.

**Why it matters.** Trivial credential discovery yields direct shell access. No exploitation expertise needed.

**Impact.** Code execution as the service account; in several cases that account has broad filesystem and network access. Provides a pivot point from the Server zone to internal infrastructure — though firewall rules in this lab block the pivot direction.

**Mitigation.** Change all default credentials, disable services that are not required (especially Telnet, rlogin, rsh), enforce key-based auth where possible, and place management interfaces behind a bastion.

---

### F-MS2-03 — Web Server Remote Code Execution (Critical, CVSS 9.8)

| Field | Value |
| ----- | ----- |
| CVSS  | 9.8 (Critical) |
| Affected services | Apache 2.2.x with vulnerable PHP, plus deliberately vulnerable web apps (Mutillidae, DVWA, etc.) |

**What it is.** The web stack has multiple unauthenticated RCE paths through outdated PHP, vulnerable web applications, and CGI exposure.

**Why it matters.** Web RCEs typically run as the web user but routinely chain into local privilege escalation on an EOL host.

**Impact.** Same end-state as the previous two: arbitrary code execution as a non-root user, with several easy LPE paths to root.

**Mitigation.** Patch the application stack, retire the host, or — for the lab — leave it as-is.

---

### F-MS2-04 — Operating System End-of-Life (High, contextual)

**What it is.** Ubuntu 8.04 (Hardy Heron) reached end-of-support in 2013 (server) / 2011 (desktop). Linux kernel 2.6 likewise.

**Why it matters.** No security patches have been issued in over a decade. Every CVE published since EOL is permanently unfixed.

**Impact.** Compounds every other finding on this host. Even if individual services were re-patched, the kernel and userland are not.

**Mitigation.** Upgrade to a currently supported release. For the lab, the EOL state is part of the exercise.

