## Domain Controller — `172.16.10.10`

### F-DC-01 — NetBIOS Name Service Open (Medium)

| Field | Value |
| ----- | ----- |
| Port  | UDP 137 |
| Severity | Medium (information disclosure → enables targeted attacks) |

**What it is.** UDP 137 (NetBIOS-NS) responds to queries, returning machine and domain naming information.

**Why it matters.** Discloses the domain name (`kbr.local`), netbios name (`KBR-DC01`), and domain role to anyone on the broadcast domain. Useful reconnaissance for an attacker preparing AS-REP roasting, Kerberoasting, or credential-stuffing attacks.

**Impact.** No direct compromise, but materially reduces the work an attacker has to do to map the AD environment.

**Mitigation.** Disable NetBIOS over TCP/IP per interface, or block UDP 137/138 and TCP 139 at the firewall. Modern Windows networks rely on DNS and do not need NetBT for AD operation.

---

### F-DC-02 — VMware Side-Channel Mitigations Disabled (Medium)

**What it is.** The hypervisor reports that side-channel mitigations (the family of controls related to Spectre, Foreshadow, MDS, etc.) are not enabled on this VM.

**Why it matters.** Co-resident VMs on the same physical host can, under specific conditions, read transient state from CPU caches and microarchitectural buffers.

**Impact.** In a multi-tenant environment, theoretical disclosure of secrets in memory across VM boundaries. In an isolated lab, low real-world risk, but worth fixing for fidelity.

**Mitigation.** In the VM's advanced settings, enable "Side-channel mitigations" (`ulm.disableMitigations = "FALSE"`). Accept the small performance cost.

