## Kali Linux — `172.16.10.200`

The host returned 2 medium-severity findings and several low/informational items. Mostly fingerprint disclosure and the inherent posture of a workstation distribution with offensive tooling installed. The most relevant non-vulnerability observation is operational: the Kali account had unrestricted sudo (`ALL:ALL`), which is convenient for lab work but not representative of a hardened workstation.

---

## Cross-Host Themes

- **Information disclosure dominates the non-target hosts.** None of the findings on the DC, Operator, or Kali would compromise the host alone, but several would meaningfully assist an attacker who already had a foothold.
- **One vulnerable VM compromises the architecture.** Even with strict segmentation, the existence of Metasploitable 2 in the lab means that an attacker landing on the Server zone (e.g., via a misconfigured firewall rule) has immediate full control of that host. Segmentation is the only thing standing between that host and the rest of the lab.
- **Configuration findings outnumber missing-patch findings.** Every meaningful finding outside Metasploitable was a configuration choice (NLA off, NetBIOS open, mitigations disabled), not a missing update. This is consistent with assessments of well-maintained but loosely-hardened environments.
